  "query_preview": "..\n\nSynoptic Setup: Forecast begins with a ridge running from northern\nManitoba into Ontario and down the Mississippi Valley. It then \nturns back west across the southern plains and into the high \npressure over the Desert SW. A broad trough over western half of \nCanada has begun to break into two parts, with the eastern trough\nrunning down the Canadian plains and into the Dakotas. The \nwestern part begins roughly above British Columbia and moves\noffshore the Pacific Northwest and Northern California. \n\nToday through Monday: Surface high pressure will move through NE\nMontana this morning, resulting in light winds. Winds will become\nE-SE this afternoon as the high moves eastward. Zonal flow aloft \nthis morning will turn WSW this afternoon due to an approaching \nshortwave. The wave will produce some rain showers and \nthunderstorms, mainly late tonight and early Monday morning along\nthe North Dakota border and Yellowstone Valley. Look for somewhat\nwarmer temperatures on Monday as the system moves to the east. \n\nMonday night into Wednesday: The western most trough will move \ninland and dig into the Intermountain West and Four Corners\nregions. In response, the northern section of the flow will build into\na ridge over Montana, keeping conditions mostly dry through\nWednesday. Tuesday specifically will need to be monitored for \nfire weather concerns due to anticipated low RH and gusty winds \nas the ridge begins to decay.\n\nWednesday night onwards: Flow aloft will attempt to become zonal \nbriefly. However, model ensembles begin to deviate on individual \nwaves within the flow. GFS runs indicate a brief ridge/trough \ncouplet, while EC members slowly orient flow more to the \nnorthwest. Standard deviations amongst ensemble members suggest \nthat no particular run is favored, so confidence in these periods\nis low. Expect changes in future iterations of the forecast from \nthis point onward. \n\nFORECAST CONFIDENCE & DEVIATIONS:\n\n- Low confidence in the western extent of rain and thunderstorm \n  chances for late tonight and early Monday morning.\n \n- Adjusted PoPs tonight at times to provide better hour-to-hour \n  consistency. No other deviations from the NBM.",
